text:
ravi: quick sanity check — i'm about to change replicas on `deployment/model-server` 3 -> 5 and add a metadata label `app.k8s.redwood/zone=west` via terraform. terraform plan is showing a replace for `kubernetes_stateful_set.redis`. anyone see why? :thinking:

jordan: hmm if plan says replace statefulset maybe the change touched a selector or volumeClaimTemplates. can you paste the plan diff? :eyes:

ravi: pasted snippet from plan: 
```
~ resource "kubernetes_deployment" "model_server" {
    spec {
      replicas = 3 -> 5
      template {
        metadata {
-         labels = {"app" = "model-server"}
+         labels = {"app" = "model-server", "app.k8s.redwood/zone" = "west"}
        }
      }
    }
  }
~ resource "kubernetes_stateful_set" "redis" {
  ~ spec {
      ~ selector { # forces replacement
          match_labels = {"app" = "redis"} -> {"app" = "redis", "app.k8s.redwood/zone" = "west"}
        }
    }
}
```

maria: ah — looks like the label ended up in the statefulset selector in your manifest. selectors are immutable for StatefulSets/Deployments; terraform will replace the resource if the selector changes. you probably added the label in a kustomize/overlay that also feeds the redis manifest.

maria: options: revert selector change, add the new label only to pod templates (metadata.labels) not to selector, or do a controlled recreate during maintenance window.

jordan: for rolling aspect: if you have to replace the statefulset, plan for PV reattach timing. if it's just the model-server deployment you're scaling, that's in-place. don't touch selectors. also consider `rollingUpdate.partition` for statefulset if you want to control which ordinals are updated first.

ravi: got it. i think i accidentally applied the label transformation across the whole k8s manifests in the terraform module. i can scope it to the model-server overlay only. two quick clarifying q's: 1) is it safe to scale model-server in-place via `kubectl scale` while i fix terraform, then reconcile with terraform later? 2) should i change deployment strategy to `maxSurge: 1` `maxUnavailable: 25%` to avoid thundering start?

jordan: 1) yes, `kubectl scale deployment/model-server --replicas=5` is fine as long as you import or let terraform reconcile later — note terraform will want to set replicas back to whatever is in code on next apply if not updated. 2) those strategy values are reasonable; we usually set `maxSurge: 1` and `maxUnavailable: 0` for low-latency services, but 25% ok for background workers.

maria: and if you *must* change the statefulset selector, do it by creating a new statefulset resource (e.g., change name) and migrate data, or plan a maintenance window. do NOT change selector in-place.

ravi: sweet — i'll patch the kustomize overlays so the `app.k8s.redwood/zone` goes under `metadata.labels` for model-server only. will kubectl scale to 5 now and hold terraform PR.

infra-ci: terraform plan for PR #4128: no changes to `kubernetes_stateful_set.redis` after overlay fix. :white_check_mark:

deploy-bot: quick note — if you scale via kubectl, add a one-line comment in the PR explaining the manual scale so oncall isn't surprised. :memo:

ravi: will do. thanks all :thumbsup:
